{"duration_ms":0,"id":22,"kind":"llm","model":"claude-opus-5","name":"claude-code.turn.14","provider":"anthropic","response":{"content":[{"text":"Done. The bug was in `config.py:17` — a bare `except Exception: return {}` that swallowed every failure and handed back empty settings, so `startup.py`'s `ConfigError` handler could never fire.\n\n`load_config` now raises `ConfigError` with a message naming the file and the underlying cause:\n\n- **Malformed JSON** → `json.JSONDecodeError` becomes `\"<path> is not valid JSON: <parser message with line/column>\"`.\n- **Unreadable/missing file** → `OSError` becomes `\"cannot read <path>: <errno detail>\"`. Same class of silent failure, so I fixed it alongside the malformed case rather than leaving half the swallow in place.\n- **Valid JSON that isn't an object** (e.g. `[1, 2]`) → rejected explicitly. Without this it would slip through, since `len()` works on a list and the app would report `started with 2 setting(s)` from a config it can't actually use.\n\nBoth raises use `from exc`, so the original traceback survives for anyone catching higher up.\n\nVerified against a live run of `startup.main` for all five cases: valid config exits 0 and prints the setting count; malformed, non-object, missing, and permission-denied each print `bad config: ...` to stderr and exit 1.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: this is a behavior change at the deploy boundary. Anything currently running with a broken or absent config file has been starting successfully on empty settings and will now fail to start. That's the point of the fix, but it means the first deploy after this change is where a latent bad config surfaces.","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_011CevGYwxS2waAw1qRtFgwu","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":231,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":231,"cache_read_input_tokens":20288,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"}},"start_ms":0,"usage":{"cache_read":20288,"cache_write_1h":231,"input":2,"output":4}}
